What is the OSI Model?

The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) Model is a way to explain how data travels from one computer to another.

Why Do We Need the OSI Model?

Imagine there were no rules for communication.

One company builds a laptop.
Another builds a router.
Another builds a server.

Without common rules, they wouldn't understand each other.

The OSI Model provides a standard way for devices to communicate.

The 7 Layers of the OSI Model

+---------------------+
| 7. Application      |
+---------------------+
| 6. Presentation     |
+---------------------+
| 5. Session          |
+---------------------+
| 4. Transport        |
+---------------------+
| 3. Network          |
+---------------------+
| 2. Data Link        |
+---------------------+
| 1. Physical         |
+---------------------+


1. Physical Layer (Layer 1)
What does it do?

This layer is responsible for sending raw bits (0s and 1s) through cables or wireless signals.

It doesn't understand websites, emails, or files.

It only sends electrical, light, or radio signals.

Devices
Ethernet Cable
Fiber Cable
Hub
Repeater
Example

When you plug an Ethernet cable into your laptop, you're using the Physical Layer.


2. Data Link Layer (Layer 2)
What does it do?

This layer makes sure data is delivered to the correct device within the same network.

It uses MAC Addresses.

Devices
Switch
Network Interface Card (NIC)
Example

In your college lab, when your laptop sends data to another laptop on the same Wi-Fi, the switch uses MAC addresses to forward it.

3. Network Layer (Layer 3)
What does it do?

This layer decides where the data should go.

It uses IP Addresses.

Devices
Router
Example

When you open YouTube, your router checks the destination IP address and forwards the packet to the internet.

4. Transport Layer (Layer 4)
What does it do?

This layer ensures that data reaches the destination correctly and completely.

It also manages ports.

Protocols
TCP
UDP
Example

When downloading a file, TCP ensures all parts arrive correctly.

During a live video call, UDP is often used because speed is more important than retransmitting lost packets.
